{"records":[{"id":"kjs2wnncjs","title":"\"Virus-host interactome and proteomic survey of PBMCs from COVID-19 patients reveal potential virulence factors influencing SARS-CoV-2 pathogenesis\" by Li et al.","description":"Supplementary Table S1-S4 for the paper entitled \"Virus-host interactome and proteomic survey of PBMCs from COVID-19 patients reveal potential virulence factors influencing SARS-CoV-2 pathogenesis\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-14T04:12:35.355Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kjs2wnncjs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kjs2wnncjs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qiming","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chao","last_name":"Peng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tongyu","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenshan","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vn3bvngtc2","title":"LC-MS/MS data of bioengineering modification of the glycome using Salmonella (Ying Sheng)","description":"We established a metabolic-engineered host cell model that generates controlled glycan structure phenotypes on the cell surface. Specifically,  we produced host cell surfaces that were primarily fucosylated, sialylated, undecorated complex type structures, and high mannose-type structures. The manipulated host cell N-glycomes were  characterized and confirmed by liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) \nanalysis. Furthermore, glycoproteomic analysis yielded site-specific N-glycosylation information. Glycopeptides were  identified and quantified using a standard glycoproteomic workflow. Collectively, our results suggested the importance of glycans in host-microbe interactions and provided a novel insight into the significance of host glycome during pathogenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-26T12:39:40.394Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vn3bvngtc2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vn3bvngtc2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ying","last_name":"Sheng","orcid":"0000-0002-6002-9783"},{"first_name":"Sheryl Joyce","last_name":"Grijaldo-Alvarez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maurice","last_name":"Wong","orcid":"0000-0003-3851-2928"},{"first_name":"Yixuan","last_name":"Xie","orcid":"0000-0002-8512-6053"},{"first_name":"Carlito","last_name":"Lebrilla","orcid":"0000-0001-7190-5323"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Tandem Mass Spectrometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xkx83byhyf","title":"Host kinesin KIF5A promotes Zika virus infection and pathogenesis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-19T08:50:43.474Z","embargo_date":"2027-08-18T23:59:59Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xkx83byhyf","data_type":"dataset","access":"embargoed","doi":["10.17632/xkx83byhyf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aleksandra","last_name":"Milewska","orcid":null}]},{"id":"sgdcys9b82","title":"Mycobacterium-Host Screening Identifies Novel Lysosomal Escape Pathways","description":"This dataset explores intracellular survival mechanisms of Mycobacterium leprae. Using clinical lesion proteomics, we identified 14 highly expressed T7SS effectors. Genome-wide yeast two-/three-hybrid screening against a macrophage interactome revealed 132 host targets and mapped the first global M. leprae effector–host interaction network. Functional validation defined two conserved lysosomal escape pathways: PPE68–Coro1A preventing phagosome–lysosome fusion, and EsxA–EsxB–CCDC115 disrupting V-ATPase assembly. Comparative M. marinum models confirmed their essential role in survival and pathogenesis. The dataset includes proteomic profiles, interaction matrices, and validation assays, which can be used to investigate host–pathogen interactions and identify therapeutic targets.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-29T07:59:18.944Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sgdcys9b82","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sgdcys9b82"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bingqing","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"xiaojie","last_name":"Liao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yongyu","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Longyun ","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rongxian","last_name":"Xie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haihong","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuzhen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoyu","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nannan","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"玮玮","last_name":"王","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhongrui","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yinjie","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peidian","last_name":"Shi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenchao","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuanqiang","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenzhen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xuechao","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianwen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhe","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zihao","last_name":"Mi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hong","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Furen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Bacterial Pathogenesis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xykyffh2xv","title":"Host Transcriptional Corepressor TLE1 is commonly exploited by RNA viruses for replication","description":"This dataset delineates alterations in host genes transcriptionally regulated by Transducin-like enhancer of split 1 (TLE1) following influenza virus infection. TLE1, a transcriptional co-repressor encoded at chromosome 9q21.32, is clinically utilized as a diagnostic marker for synovial sarcoma. However, our analysis reveals that TLE1-regulated gene sets identified by ChIP enrichment are distinct post-infection, indicating its previously uncharacterized role in modulating transcriptional reprogramming during viral challenge. These findings provide novel insights for identifying host-directed antiviral targets and elucidate mechanisms by which influenza virus subverts host gene expression to facilitate replication and pathogenesis, thereby highlighting potential therapeutic interventions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-17T13:54:13.077Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xykyffh2xv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xykyffh2xv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiaomeng","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haoran","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peng","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"kailai","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuofeng","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Influenza Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8y5wm8887b","title":"Profile hidden Markov models for the identification of DNMT and TET homologs in insects","description":"Profile hidden Markov models built from DNMT and TET arthropod sequences. Orthologous sequences of each DNMT and TET were downloaded from OrthoDB(http://www.orthodb.org/).\n\n1. DNMT1: DNA methyltransferase 1\n2. TRDMT1:  tRNA aspartic acid methyltransferase 2 (also known as DNA methyltransferase 2 or DNMT2)\n3. DNMT3: DNA methyltransferase 3\n4. TET: Ten-eleven translocation methylcytosine dioxygenase","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-03-21T19:26:52.012Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8y5wm8887b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8y5wm8887b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Panagiotis","last_name":"Provataris","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hidden Markov Models"],"journal_issn":["17596653"]},{"id":"57j37g6kdh","title":"High-resolution photocatalytic mapping of SARS-CoV-2 spike interactions on the cell surface. Datta et al","description":"Identifying virus-host interactions on the cell surface can improve our understanding of viral entry and pathogenesis. SARS-CoV-2, the causative agent of the COVID-19 disease, uses ACE2 as a receptor to enter cells. Yet the full repertoire of cell surface proteins that contribute to viral entry is unknown. We developed a photocatalyst-based viral-host protein microenvironment mapping platform (ViraMap) to probe the molecular neighborhood of the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein on the human cell surface. Application of ViraMap to ACE2-expressing cells captured ACE2, the established co-receptor NRP1, and several novel cell surface proteins. We systematically analyzed the relevance of these candidate proteins to SARS-CoV-2 entry by knockdown and overexpression approaches in pseudovirus and authentic infection models and identified PTGFRN and EFNB1 as bona fide viral entry factors. Our results highlight additional host targets that participate in SARS-CoV-2 infection and showcase ViraMap as a powerful platform for defining viral interactions on the cell surface.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-07T06:19:10.123Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/57j37g6kdh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/57j37g6kdh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mohsan","last_name":"Saeed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suprama","last_name":"Datta","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ph3dkb947t","title":"Intestinal host-microbe interactions fuel pulmonary inflammation in cigarette smoke exposed mice","description":"The gut microbiota has been implicated in numerous aspects of host health and immune regulation. Yet, recent studies have linked gut microbes to the pathogenesis of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), however, the underlying mechanisms remain elusive. Here, we investigated the role of gastrointestinal (GI) host-microbe interactions on pulmonary health. Using two distinct means of modulating GI host-microbe relations, we dissected how gut microbes fuel pulmonary inflammation in mouse models of cigarette smoke (CS)-induced lung damage. We found that CS caused profound changes to the colonic mucosa, with reduced mucus and increased bacterial encroachment. Modulating host-microbe interactions using antibiotics and recombinant human β-defensin 2 restricted colonic bacterial encroachment, limiting interactions between host and microbe. These strategies resulted in substantial ~50% decrease in pulmonary neutrophil infiltration following both acute and chronic exposure to CS. These findings provide additional evidence of a gut-lung axis, offering novel insight into the role of the gut microbiota in COPD, which could represent a novel avenue for future therapeutic strategies. OTU tables are deposited with informative sample ID, and raw data from each figure panel is compiled in an excel metafile with clearly labelled sheets. Each sheet contains information about project ID, cage ID, mouse ID, Treatment, and experimental day. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-25T10:22:36.339Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ph3dkb947t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ph3dkb947t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sune Kjærsgaard","last_name":"Yang-Jensen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Lung","Gut Microbiota","Cigarette Smoking","Microbiota"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxn8cv2d2h","title":"Inhibition of the m6A mRNA methyltransferase complex by the alphaherpesvirus viral protein kinase US3 ","description":"Chemical modifications of mRNA, the so-called epitranscriptome, have emerged as an additional layer of post-transcriptional regulation of gene expression. The most common epitranscriptomic modification, m6A, is generated by a large multi-subunit complex. Although the components that make up the m6A methyltransferase complex have been identified, it is currently largely unclear how the activity of this complex is regulated. Here we show that alphaherpesvirus kinases phosphorylate several components of the m6A methyltransferase complex, including METTL3, METTL14 and WTAP. Phosphorylation of these proteins correlates with inhibition of the m6A methyltransferase complex and a near complete loss of m6A levels in alphaherpesvirus-infected cells. Expression of the viral serine/threonine protein kinase US3 is necessary and sufficient for phosphorylation of METTL3 and METTL14 and inhibition of the m6A methyltransferase complex. Together, these findings provide the first evidence for phosphorylation that inactivates the m6A methyltransferase complex, in this case mediated by the viral US3 kinase. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-20T08:03:21.955Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxn8cv2d2h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxn8cv2d2h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Jansens","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","RNA"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":117293,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":124,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":117293,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":117025,"restricted":58,"embargoed":210},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2115},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":583},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":456},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":404},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":395},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":381},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":348},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":288},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":261},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":210}],"